        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Mënsch (Luxembourgish Trick-Taking)',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "MÃ«nsch is a traditional trick-taking game from Luxembourg, belonging to the Ombre/Solo family of European card games. Played by four players with a 32-card deck, it features the 'Mensch' (the Queen of Clubs) as the permanent highest trump card, regardless of the declared suit. It is a game of shifting alliances where a bidder can choose to play alone (Solo) or call a partner to help them win a majority of tricks.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Derived from the ancient Spanish game 'Hombre' (L'Homme), which spread through Europe and evolved into regional variants like German Solo and Luxembourgish MÃ«nsch.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players (individuals).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32 cards. Remove 2-6 from a standard deck.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32 cards. Remove 2-6 from a standard deck.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player receives 8 cards (dealt in batches of 4).',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'No-bid pays two chips: Some play that if no one bids, the holder of the Queen of Clubs names trumps and calls a partner; if the calling team takes four tricks or fewer, the caller and partner each pay two chips (same as Half/After) instead of one.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
